  "text": "To solve the problem, we start by letting \\( z = x + yi \\), where \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are real numbers and \\( i \\) is the imaginary unit. We need to find the smallest possible value of \\( |z - 2i| \\) given that:\n\n\\[ |z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|. \\]\n\nFirst, we express \\( z^2 \\) and \\( z(z - 3i) \\) in terms of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ z^2 = (x + yi)^2 = x^2 + 2xyi + (yi)^2 = x^2 - y^2 + 2xyi, \\]\n\\[ z(z - 3i) = (x + yi)(x - (3 + y)i) = x^2 - x(3 + y)i + xyi + y(3 + y)i = x^2 - x(3 + y)i + (xy + y)(3 + y)i = x^2 - x(3 + y)i + (3y + y^2 + xy)i. \\]\n\nWe then rewrite the given equation \\( |z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)| \\) in terms of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ z^2 - 9 = (x^2 - y^2 - 9) + 2xyi, \\]\n\\[ z(z - 3i) = (x^2 - x(3 + y) + (3y + y^2 + xy)i. \\]\n\nThe magnitudes of these complex numbers are:\n\n\\[ |z^2 - 9| = \\sqrt{(x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 + (2xy)^2}, \\]\n\\[ |z(z - 3i)| = \\sqrt{x^4 - x^2(3 + y) + x^2y + (3y + y^2 + xy)^2}. \\]\n\nEquating these magnitudes, we get:\n\n\\[ (x^2 - y^2 - 9)^2 + (2xy)^2 = x^4 - x^2(3 + y) + x^2y + (3y + y^2 + xy)^2. \\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we can see that the condition holds when \\( z = 2i \\), because:\n\n\\[ |(2i)^2 - 9| = |2i(2i - 3i)| = |-8| = 8, \\]\n\\[ |4 - 9| = |4 - 9| = |-5| = 5. \\]\n\nThis is consistent. Now, we need to find the smallest possible value of \\( |z - 2i| \\). If \\( z \\) is on the line of the constant imaginary parts, we can imagine \\( z \\) is moving along the imaginary axis like \\( z = bi \\) for some real number \\( b \\). Then:\n\n\\[ |bi - 2i| = |(b - 2)i| = |b - 2|. \\]\n\nThe smallest value of \\( |b - 2| \\) is when \\( b = 2 \\), which is 0. The value is the smallest when \\( z \\) is adjusted such that the magnitudes are invariant and the difference minimized.\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( |z - 2i| \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{2}. \\]",
